WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.000
Elizabeth Bennet. Emma Woodhouse. Marianne&nbsp;
Dashwood. Jane Austen has been responsible

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.880
for creating some of the most frequently adapted&nbsp;
and analyzed women in the English literary canon.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:19.120
Along with BuzzFeed quizzes asking “which&nbsp;
SATC or Little Women” character you are,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.360
there is always fanfare about&nbsp;
which Jane Austen heroine you are.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:30.880
But beyond the big three. Well mostly … big two.&nbsp;
Who are the women of Jane Austen’s completed

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.560
novels? How do they reveal to us, her modern&nbsp;
audience, any insight into her growth as an

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:43.680
author, her politics, and just how she feels&nbsp;
about what makes a girlboss and a #girlboss.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.160
This is Austen lady’s night. Oh, What a Night.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.640
Let’s go in reverse cool girl order and start with

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.440
Fanny Price, the heroine of Austen’s&nbsp;
second-longest novel, Mansfield Park.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.880
Unlike a lot of Austen’s other protagonists,&nbsp;
Fanny is poor-poor, and is only spared an orphan’s

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.880
existence because her aunt married well. She is&nbsp;
brought in as the poor cousin to the rich Bertram

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.320
family to be a companion to their daughters. All&nbsp;
the while, Fanny is constantly being reminded by

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:25.120
everyone, especially her aunt Mrs. Norris, that&nbsp;
she is here as charity. She doesn’t belong.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.960
Well, everyone but her cousin Edmund who&nbsp;
is her love interest in the story. And yes,

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:39.840
I said cousin. First cousin. But it’s regency-era&nbsp;
England and they are pretty okay with that.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.280
Fanny is not an active player in her&nbsp;
story. Years of being told she is nothing,

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.880
has done immense damage to her self-esteem. As&nbsp;
a result, the reader is constantly in her head,

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.280
frustrated at how little mobility&nbsp;
Fanny has to change her fate.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.440
In the journal, The Eighteenth&nbsp;
Century, George Haggerty says of Fanny:

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.080
“Austen creates a heroine who stands apart&nbsp;
from the central action of the narrative.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.040
None of her other heroines behave this way,&nbsp;
but then none of them is wrenched out of her

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.960
home and forced to come to terms with a family in&nbsp;
which she is not fully welcome. [...] The novel

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:21.440
shows nothing more vividly than it shows Fanny's&nbsp;
mettle; for even when she is most frustrating,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.640
she is struggling to be herself in a world&nbsp;
that is distinctly un-friendly. In a sense,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.880
the real action of the novel takes place in&nbsp;
Fanny's responses to everything around her,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.400
for if she is shunted off&nbsp;
to the side of what seems

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.280
at times like the main action of the novel,&nbsp;
then so are the readers who attend to her.”

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.320
What makes Fanny interesting from a character&nbsp;
standpoint is she is a victim of emotional

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.560
abuse. She has been mistreated so deeply but&nbsp;
remains almost martyr-like in her devotion.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.680
We also see that the Bertrams may be rich, but&nbsp;
have no moral fortitude which might be Austen’s

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.600
own political commentary since it is established&nbsp;
that part of their money comes from slavery.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.680
Clara Calvo, professor of English at University&nbsp;
of Murcia (mur-SEE-a), has called Fanny “priggish”

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.880
but I think what makes Fanny so unlikable&nbsp;
is that she isn’t Gilmore Girls poor.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.800
A noble, wish-fulfillment poor where&nbsp;
you’ve somehow pulled yourself up,

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:25.840
are endlessly witty and have&nbsp;
your pick of fine suitors.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:33.440
Fanny is broken and does not have the mobility,&nbsp;
money, or opportunities to have more in her life.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.640
She can only endure because that&nbsp;
is what she has been trained to do.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.880
It’s not fun, but it’s real.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:46.880
Continuing our class discourse there is&nbsp;
Anne Elliot the heroine of Persuasion.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.880
Persuasion was the final completed&nbsp;
novel to be published by Austen

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.800
and in it you see the continued&nbsp;
evolution of Austen as a writer.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:02.400
Anne Elliot is 27-years-old when we meet her,&nbsp;
unmarried. So she is basically a #spinster

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.320
to everyone around her. We find out that she&nbsp;
was once engaged to marry a man named Frederick

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:14.080
Wentworth, a young fine common naval&nbsp;
officer. Who was clever, but poor.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.240
Sadly, the people in her&nbsp;
life had never seen Aladdin

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.720
and persuaded (heh-heh), her&nbsp;
to break off the engagement.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:29.600
Now, nearly a decade later, Wentworth is a&nbsp;
self-made rich hottie and a Navy Captain.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:34.720
So now Anne is singing Sk8er Boi to herself&nbsp;
watching the guy she used to knowwwww

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:36.880
be hot and cool.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.200
Also, is Sk8er Boi an adaptation of&nbsp;
Persuasion? Let us know in the comments below.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:49.920
Classwise, Austen tears the hereditary aristocracy&nbsp;
to shreds and portrays its men as either villains,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:57.920
wasteful or vain, in contrast to Wentworth&nbsp;
who created his own money. Unlike the rest

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:04.160
of Austen’s heroines, it is Anne who comes&nbsp;
from money and her partner is lower class.

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:12.720
Unlike Darcy or Edmund or any of the other&nbsp;
men in Austen's novels, Wentworth isn’t a savor from poverty.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:18.240
He is a savior from blind elitism. And&nbsp;
through Austen’s very insular writing of Anne

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.920
we get a character, who is considered&nbsp;
to be the most perceptive heroine.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:30.160
As Harvard Professor Deidre Shauna Lynch explains,&nbsp;
“… [Austen] casts her protagonists as the silent

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:35.600
and sympathetic observers of other people’s&nbsp;
stories and the repositories of their secrets

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:39.680
Their participation in situations&nbsp;
of spectatorship and secret-sharing

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:45.360
endows Anne Elliot and Elinor Dashwood, in&nbsp;
particular, with the qualities that novel

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:51.600
readers in Austen’s lifetime were learning to&nbsp;
associate with third-person narrators; likewise

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:58.160
the capacity that each has for a self-possession&nbsp;
that seems to numb her sense of self-interest.”

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:04.800
Like Fanny, Anne is not a sexy heroine, but she&nbsp;
represents the growth of Austen’s authorship.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.280
Her work was very class conscious and&nbsp;
these two books have some of her most

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.760
thoughtful commentary on class, wealth, and love.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:20.640
… And then you get Northanger Abbey. Northanger&nbsp;
Abbey is a satirical coming of age novel

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:26.560
that pokes fun at gothic novels, but&nbsp;
let's be real here—it's a YA novel.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:32.640
Our leading lady, Catherine Morland, is a&nbsp;
“heroine in training” who loves gothic novels

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:38.160
a lot. Due to her lack of real life&nbsp;
perspective--coupled with a rampant imagination

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.800
she creates realities in her mind in order to&nbsp;
fill in the gaps within her own social naivety.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.320
Catherine is … fine. She’s kind of boring&nbsp;
because her story doesn’t have that many stakes.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.520
She’s just silly and eventually stops being silly.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:03.520
If anything what makes Abbey a fun novel is seeing&nbsp;
how petty Jane Austen felt about the gothic novel

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:10.640
with it’s overly perfect characters. Catherine is&nbsp;
made plain and middle class. Her life interest,

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:17.440
Henry Tilney, is a passive soft boy instead&nbsp;
of a handsome brave hyper-masculine man.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.560
It is interesting as&nbsp;
literature, but not as a story.

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.680
The Big Three of Jane Austen novels are Emma,

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:32.080
Pride &amp; Prejudice, and Sense &amp; Sensibility. If&nbsp;
they were 90s/2000s teen movies they would be

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.400
Jawbreaker, Mean Girls,&nbsp;
and Clueless, respectively.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:41.920
Emma is the Jawbreaker of the big&nbsp;
three of the Jane Austen novel

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:45.840
because while it is a teen classic,&nbsp;
it is a little bit more cutthroat.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.520
Especially because while people might find&nbsp;
Fanny boring and Anne a little too clean cut,

00:07:54.960 --> 00:08:00.640
Emma is kind of a mean girl and that has&nbsp;
been rubbing people wrong since 1815.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:04.720
And yes, I know that Clueless is based on Emma,

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.600
but for the sake of this&nbsp;
argument the point is moot.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:15.120
Emma is rich, in fact the novel begins&nbsp;
with saying that Emma Woodhouse is

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:21.360
“handsome, clever, and rich, with a comfortable&nbsp;
home and a happy disposition... and had lived

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:26.240
nearly twenty-one years in the world&nbsp;
with very little to distress or vex her."

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.160
Throughout the novel, convincing herself&nbsp;
that she is an apt matchmaker attempts to

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:39.920
manipulate the love life of her friend Harriet&nbsp;
Smith. However, due to her elitism, she almost

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:45.600
ruins Harriet’s own chance for happiness when&nbsp;
the girl falls in love with a handsome farmer,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.440
who Emma deems not socially&nbsp;
good enough for her friend.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.400
Jane Austen even said that with Emma&nbsp;
she wanted to create a heroine that

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.960
only she would like. Emma is spoiled,&nbsp;
over-confident, and likes to meddle in

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.560
the lives of people like dolls because she&nbsp;
simply has the standing in society to do so.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:08.800
But that is intentional.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.680
As Austen Scholar, John Mullan, so aptly&nbsp;
puts it concerning Emma’s snobbery:

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:20.000
“Of course she is. But Austen, with a refusal&nbsp;
of moralism worthy of Flaubert, abandons her

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:26.320
protagonist to her snobbery and confidently risks&nbsp;
inciting foolish readers to think that the author

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:32.400
must be a snob too. Emma’s snobbery pervades the&nbsp;
novel, from that moment when we hear Mrs Goddard,

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:38.400
the mistress of the little girls’ boarding school,&nbsp;
and Mrs and Miss Bates described as “the most

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:43.840
come-at-able” denizens of Highbury (meaning that&nbsp;
they are at the beck and call of Emma and her

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:50.240
hypochondriac father). Austen has the integrity to&nbsp;
make Emma snobbish even when she is in the right.”

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:54.160
This might come as a shock but, female characters

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:58.880
are allowed to be unlikable. Character&nbsp;
development is good actually.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:13.280
Sense &amp; Sensibility is Austen’s first novel and&nbsp;
it is a very good one. It gave us the Dashwoods,

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:17.440
and the two important sisters of&nbsp;
the novel, Elinor and Marianne

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:20.960
who encompass the titular Sense &amp; Sensibility.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:28.960
Elinor is a pragmatic softy who tries to hold&nbsp;
it together, but she is brimming with lust

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:35.040
and just wants to hold hands very much with&nbsp;
Edward Ferrars. In the novel she finds out

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:43.200
Edward made a promise to marry Lucy Steele when he&nbsp;
was younger and forces himself to give up Elinor.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.160
Both make the noble decisions and are&nbsp;
rewarded for it with a happily ever after.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:07.280
Marianne meets a bad boy named John Willoughby,&nbsp;
she falls in love with f-boy, but later finds out

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:15.120
that he’s not that innocent. He is a rake who has&nbsp;
already seduced a 15-year-old girl, then abandoned

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:24.640
her although she was pregnant. He was cut off from&nbsp;
his family and therefore in need of a rich wife.

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:31.040
He loves Marianne, but she’s broke, so he&nbsp;
abandons her. This leads to Marianne becoming

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.520
distraught but eventually she&nbsp;
returns the affections of the older,

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.600
more stable, and secretly&nbsp;
low-key Byronic, Colonel Brandon.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:48.240
This ending has been seen as&nbsp;
controversial for some as some

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:51.680
feel Marianne is shamed into not loving as deeply.

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.560
A lot of fanfic explores the idea&nbsp;
of Willoughby being redeemed,

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:01.360
why is that explored, and&nbsp;
even hinted at—but not canon?

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:10.880
Cultural historian and literary critic, Mary&nbsp;
Poovey, makes the following point: “Consistently,

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:16.400
Elinor makes the prudent choice, even when&nbsp;
doing so is painful; almost as consistently,

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:21.680
Marianne’s decisions are self-indulgent and&nbsp;
harmful, either to herself or to someone

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:27.680
else. But this neat design is less stable than an&nbsp;
absolute and authoritative moral system would seem

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:34.720
to require. Many readers have found Marianne’s&nbsp;
“spirit” more appealing than Elinor’s cautious,

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:40.240
prim, and even repressive reserve, and&nbsp;
they have found Marianne’s passionate

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:46.320
romance with Willoughby more attractive than the&nbsp;
prolonged frustration to which Elinor submits.”

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:52.640
Marianne’s passion and young love is engaging and&nbsp;
beautiful in some ways. Especially since she does

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:56.640
meet a great love, but the moral&nbsp;
is not that loving deeply is bad.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:03.040
Instead it is more that love without&nbsp;
respect and morality means nothing.

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:08.560
Which brings us to … Elizabeth Bennet,&nbsp;
the Final Boss of Jane Austen heroines.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:14.960
Lizzy THE Bennet, the protagonist of Jane&nbsp;
Austen’s second novel, Pride and Prejudice.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:21.440
Published in 1813, Pride and Prejudice concerns&nbsp;
itself with the fortunes and misfortunes of the

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:28.080
Bennet family; the family’s patriarch, Mr. Bennet,&nbsp;
is a gentleman farmer with a modest income of 2000

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:34.800
pounds a year and five daughters—meaning none&nbsp;
of his children can legally inherit any of it,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:41.567
because, you guessed it, systemic misogyny. You've seen Downton Abbey, you know how it goes.

00:13:41.567 --> 00:13:47.019
Likewise, Mrs. Bennet has no fortune to her name, and thus the predicament that spurs on the&nbsp;
plot: 

00:13:47.019 --> 00:13:54.960
Will the Bennet sisters be able to find suitors rich enough to care for them before&nbsp;their father dies?

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.800
Enter Lizzy Bennet, the sarcastic,&nbsp;
high-spirited second daughter,

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:03.600
who watches as her elder sister Jane&nbsp;
falls for the gentle Mr. Bingley,

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:09.760
a wealthy bachelor that has moved into a&nbsp;
nearby estate—-who, much to Lizzy’s chagrin,

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:15.840
seems to always be accompanied by his&nbsp;
grumpy and blunt friend, Fitzwilliam Darcy.

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:22.720
Much of the novel follows the constant&nbsp;
misunderstandings and reconciliations

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:28.720
between Lizzy and Mr. Darcy as they grow close&nbsp;
to one another, while family and society seem to

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:34.240
do everything they can to keep them apart—whether&nbsp;
it’s Mrs. Bennet’s attempts to marry Lizzy to her

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:42.320
male cousin set to inherit the Bennet estate (ew,&nbsp;
mom), Mr. Bingley’s vain sister who also has her

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:49.520
eye on Mr. Darcy, or Mr. Darcy’s cold aunt, who&nbsp;
would have him marry her sickly daughter Anne.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:58.800
Lizzy is the most popular Austen heroine.&nbsp;
Why? Is it because she made Darcy into a simp?

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:05.600
Is it because she has the best bars? Is it because&nbsp;
she gives lower-middle-class brown haired girls

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:14.640
hope they can marry into wealth? Not unlike&nbsp;
Elinor, Lizzy’s wellbeing is outwardly presented

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:19.920
as solely dependent on good marriage and her&nbsp;
love for someone above her social standing.

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.120
Says Jane Austen scholar, Claudia L. Johnson:

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:27.680
In all of Austen’s novels, but especially&nbsp;
in Pride and Prejudice, pursuing happiness

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:33.120
is the business of life. Austen trots out&nbsp;
character after character before our attention

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:38.720
so that we may consider what pleases,&nbsp;
or conversely, vexes and mortifies them,

00:15:38.720 --> 00:15:43.680
thus inviting us to assess the quality&nbsp;
and durability of their happiness.

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:50.080
What sets Lizzy apart from other Austen heroines&nbsp;
her absolute disregard for the mores of courtship

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:56.480
and station that would have added to Elinor’s&nbsp;
distress—Lizzy doggedly listens to no one—she

00:15:56.480 --> 00:16:01.680
spurns the snobbery of Miss Bingley, bites&nbsp;
back at Mr. Darcy’s aunt when she is mocked.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:07.600
She is not gifted with the station Emma has, nor&nbsp;
does she have any taste for social engineering.

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:11.920
While it still ultimately has to bend&nbsp;
to early 19th century conventions,

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:15.360
Lizzy is the mistress of her own&nbsp;
happiness in Pride and Prejudice

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:21.840
in a way that her other beloved characters&nbsp;
or not, and the novel asks us to consider

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:26.960
what happiness means in a world with limited&nbsp;
opportunity. To an extend, all of the Bennet

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:33.040
sisters go through this journey—Lydia’s scandalous&nbsp;
love affair vs what society would say of it,

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:39.760
quiet, moralizing Mary and her disinterest in love&nbsp;
games, and Jane, who’s happy ending is dangled in

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:44.080
front of her from the very beginning and might&nbsp;
have been the protagonist in any other book.

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:49.120
And then there’s…. Mrs. Bennet. Do&nbsp;
we have time to discuss Mrs.Bennet?

00:16:50.960 --> 00:16:55.840
Do I have the mental energy to discuss&nbsp;
whether or not she’s a villainous caricature

00:16:55.840 --> 00:17:00.800
or a misunderstood mother doing the best she&nbsp;can? No I do not.

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:04.080
Academic and writer Janet Todd&nbsp;
condenses the eternal appeal

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:04.880
of the ladies as Jane Austen best, saying:

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:10.560
“...what strikes me as contributing most&nbsp;
to Jane Austen’s universal popularity

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:13.760
[is] her ability to create the&nbsp;
illusion of psychologically

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:19.440
believable and self-reflecting characters.&nbsp;
Her novels are investigations of selfhood,

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:24.000
particularly female, the oscillating&nbsp;
relationship of feeling and reason,

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:30.320
the interaction of present and memory, and the&nbsp;constant negotiation between desire and society.”

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:33.520
Inasmuch as the world has changed&nbsp;
over the last two hundred years,

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:39.200
along with societal expectations of how women are&nbsp;supposed to be have and what roles and dreams they

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:44.560
can aspire to, the conflict between duty and&nbsp;self-fulfillment will always be present—and

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:49.120
so, the eternal appeal of dance cards,&nbsp;
rainy encounters with the love of your life,

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:52.880
and the enduring spirit of Jane&nbsp;
Austen’s characters remains.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:57.840
When you look at the heroines of Jane Austen’s&nbsp;
books you see the evolution of a fiercely

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:03.600
independent author building upon the foundation&nbsp;
of female authors before her (shout out to Frances

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:14.320
Burney who never appeared on the show. But I love. And she is great. Wikipedia her). Austen died at 41. If she had lived&nbsp;
who knows what kind of heroines she would have

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:18.560
created. What politics would she&nbsp;
have explored? But in six novels

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:24.640
she has delivered the stories of six amazing&nbsp;
women who make up the most amazing Buzzfeed

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:29.840
personality quiz that everyone cheats at&nbsp;
anyway to get Lizzy Bennet in the end.

